## 3. A bounded current from the full primitives

For a bounded finite sum of matter-gated rotor-shift words X, define on the physical core

\[
\partial_e X=[E_e,X].
\]

Each shift word changes E_e by a fixed finite integer, while its diagonal gates commute with E_e. Thus this commutator extends to a bounded operator for X=H4 and X=B_mu. This does not require E_e itself to be bounded, controlled by D, or integrable in the state. Adjoints satisfy partial_e(X^*)=-(partial_e X)^*.

Define positive current as charge transport along the orientation A to B:

\[
J_e=i\delta\,\partial_eH_4
 -\frac{\kappa}{2}\sum_\mu
    \left[B_\mu^*\partial_eB_\mu
                 +(\partial_eB_\mu)^*B_\mu\right].                \tag{4}
\]

This is bounded and self-adjoint. On the core it is minus the formal Heisenberg image of E_e. In particular an outward hop of positive charge produces positive current, because it decreases E on that edge. Formula (4), not an expectation of an unbounded electric link, is the definition used for general normal states. The current is a natural representative selected by the electric links; the divergence equation alone would allow addition of a divergence-free current.

There are explicit local bounds. For e=(a,b), the color-resolved single-edge outward hop gives ||partial_e F_a||<=1: its two old-color pieces have orthogonal domains and ranges. For an overlapping pair containing a, ||S_ac||<=d_a d_c and ||partial_e S_ac||<=d_c. Other pair terms have zero derivative. Therefore

\[
\|\partial_eH_4\|\le4d_a\sum_{c\ne a:\,N(c)\cap N(a)\ne\varnothing}d_c^2.
\]

For a resolved channel on the marked edge ab, partial_e B_(ab,sigma)=sigma B_(ab,sigma). On another edge ac of that star the derivative has norm at most one, and it is zero outside the star. Using ||B_(av,sigma)||<=d_a-1 gives

\[
\|J_e\|\le4\delta d_a\sum_{c:\,c\ne a,\,N(c)\cap N(a)\ne\varnothing}d_c^2
                    +4\kappa(d_a-1)^2.                           \tag{5}
\]

The same bound holds for the coherent instrument. For a fixed marked edge, B_+ and B_- have orthogonal final A-charge ranges. The derivatives partial_e B_sigma have the same final charge ranges, so B_+^* partial_e B_-=B_-^* partial_e B_+=0. Consequently the dissipative part of (4) is exactly the same bounded operator for either instrument. This does not identify their complete density evolutions: coherent jump gains can retain off-diagonal output charge terms, and the later state can differ.

For maximum degree z, the Hamiltonian part of (5) is at most 4 delta z^4(z-1), independent of electric amplitudes. Local boundedness is being stated; no infinite-volume semigroup or volume-uniform microscopic transfer is inferred from it.

## 4. Exact continuity and domains without electric moments

Let s_xe be +1 if x is the A endpoint, -1 if it is the B endpoint, and zero otherwise. Gauge preservation gives on the physical core

\[
[Q_x,X]=\sum_e s_{xe}\partial_e X,\qquad X=H_4,B_\mu.
\]

Both sides extend boundedly, so the identity holds as a bounded-operator equality. The electric term commutes strongly with every Q_x. Substitution into the Hamiltonian commutator and dissipator gives

\[
A_{Q_x}:=\mathcal L^*(Q_x)=-\sum_e s_{xe}J_e,
\qquad
A_{R_f}=-\sum_{e=(a,b)}(f_a-f_b)J_e.                          \tag{6}
\]

The notation L^* here denotes the bounded expression defined by the common law; it is not a claim that every unbounded electric observable belongs to a normal generator domain.

For precision, write the trace-class generator as the unitary KD generator plus its bounded remainder V. The parent mild solution obeys the variation-of-constants identity with the strongly continuous trace-class group U_D(t). Any bounded diagonal charge function X strongly commutes with D. Pairing the mild identity with X eliminates U_D exactly:

\[
\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_tX)-\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_0X)
 =\int_0^t\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_s A_X)\,ds,                 \tag{7}
\]

where A_X=i delta[H4,X]+kappa sum D^*[B_mu](X) is bounded. The integrand is continuous because rho_s is trace-norm continuous. Thus the expectation is continuously differentiable (right differentiable at zero) for every normal initial state. This applies to Q_x, R_f and R_f^*R_g without electric, energy or fourth-moment assumptions. It does not require norm continuity of the electric conjugation on all bounded rotor operators.

Combining (6)-(7) yields actual finite-time mean charge continuity,

\[
\langle R_f\rangle_t-\langle R_f\rangle_0
 =-\int_0^t\sum_{e=(a,b)}(f_a-f_b)\langle J_e\rangle_s\,ds.   \tag{8}
\]

One must not replace this by a difference of undefined means of E_e when the state has no electric moment. All color coherences, Gauss-allowed circulating fields and winding sectors are included. For constant f, R_f=0 on physical space and the current divergence sums to zero identically.

