## 6. A controlled fixed-graph initial expansion

No extra domain regularity is needed for a quantitative time remainder. Starting in P0, the number-diagonal state remains number-diagonal; h and the loss preserve N and each jump raises N by two. There is no gain back into P0. By (11), its exact surviving block is the unitary first-sector field evolution times exp(-lambda0 t). Thus its trace is exp(-lambda0 t), for every normal initial field.

For X=R_f or X=R_f^*R_g, let a_X be (1) or (2). The bounded A_X preserves number and satisfies P0 A_X P0=a_X P0. Consequently

\[
|\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_s A_X)-a_X|
\le (|a_X|+\|A_X\|)(1-e^{-\lambda_0s})
\le\lambda_0(|a_X|+\|A_X\|)s.
\]

Integrating (7) proves

\[
|\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_tX)-a_Xt|
\le\tfrac12\lambda_0(|a_X|+\|A_X\|)t^2.                    \tag{14}
\]

Also |mu_f(t)|<=||A_Rf||t. Therefore the connected covariance differs from the right side of (2) times t by at most

\[
\left[\tfrac12\lambda_0(|a_{fg}|+\|A_{R_f^*R_g}\|)
                  +\|A_{R_f}\|\|A_{R_g}\|\right]t^2.       \tag{15}
\]

These are finite-graph, fixed-test bounds uniform over normal initial rotor states. They are not volume-uniform: lambda0 and the observable/remainder norms can grow with the graph. If lambda0=0, the initial sector remains and all these charge moments stay zero. No assertion is made about differentiability of rho_t itself in trace norm at zero or of an unbounded energy expectation.

